  <section class="etched">
    <div class="etched-grid solo">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>V1: tiny computers, by the rack.</h2>
        <p>The cell above is a concept. V1 is built around the board already on our bench, the AMD Ryzen AI Max+ 395 with 128 GB of memory, in a shell of our own: narrow, deep and tall, 60 by 240 by 120 millimetres, the board standing on edge, the heatsink's fins running front to back, a grille the full front, two fans at the rear beside the ports. That is the node. It breathes front to back on its own, so one stands on a desk as the local-hosting product and six stand side by side in a tray. Fourteen trays, eighty-four nodes, make a pod. Pods in a row, with a cold aisle in front and a hot aisle behind, are the cluster. The shell and its heatsink are the only custom parts.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="etched">
    <div class="etched-grid">
      <figure><img src="../assets/img/node-open.webp" alt="The node with its side off: the board on edge, the package under a finned heatsink with the fins running front to back, two fans at the rear" loading="lazy"></figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The node.</h2>
        <p>It runs a 7B at eight requests at once under a capped clock at about 190 tokens per second and 111 joules per correct answer, the measured figure for its package, and a 32B fits in its memory. What is not measured yet is the whole box at the wall, its blowers and supply included, under the cap; that is a plug-in meter and an afternoon, and it is first on the list.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="etched">
    <div class="etched-grid">
      <figure><img src="../assets/img/node-grid.webp" alt="Four nodes docked into a two by two grid, side by side and stacked" loading="lazy"></figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>Why this shape, and the grid.</h2>
        <p>The most efficient box for air is the one where air goes in one face, along the fins, and out the opposite face with no turn: the least fan power per watt removed, every fin in the stream, and a box that packs edge to edge on its closed faces. The board standing on edge sets the depth, the heatsink sets the height, and the width is the board plus the fins plus the fan. So the node is narrow, deep and tall.</p>
        <p>Each side and the top and bottom carry a recessed blind-mate dock with two alignment pins: power passes through and the link is a 10 gigabit pair. Nothing is on the front or the rear, so the air path never changes. Push two nodes together and they are one machine; four make a 2 by 2 grid on a desk, 32B class with headroom, on one power lead and one network cable.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="etched">
    <div class="etched-grid">
      <figure><img src="../assets/img/node-tray.webp" alt="A 3U tray holding six nodes standing side by side with a fan plate behind" loading="lazy"></figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The tray: six across.</h2>
        <p>The bought box breathes through its top and sides and cannot be packed; the Spliced shell breathes front to back and can. A 3U tray at standard rack width holds six nodes standing side by side, 12 millimetres apart, one row deep, each pulling its own air through the tray, with a fan plate at the rear that empties the plenum. No host board: every node is its own host. The tray carries power distribution and a badge; each node's 10 gigabit port cables to the pod's switch.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="etched">
    <div class="etched-grid">
      <figure><img src="../assets/img/node-rack.webp" alt="A 42U rack of fourteen trays, eighty-four nodes, with a power shelf at the bottom and switches at the top" loading="lazy"></figure>
      <div class="prose">
        <h2>The pod, and what the budget says.</h2>
        <p>Fourteen trays, eighty-four nodes, a power shelf, two switches: about 7.6 kilowatts busy on the measured per-node package figure plus a placeholder for each node's fans and supply and the tray fans, and about 16,000 tokens per second on a 7B. The budget in our scheduler passes its airflow, feed and idle checks on paper. It also says the design's first fight is everything around the node: fans, the switch, conversion losses and idle are nearly as much power as the nodes themselves at low load. The row of pods below sits in hot-aisle containment like any row.</p>
        <h3>What would have to be true, in order of cost</h3>
        <p>The node's wall power under the cap, idle and busy: a plug-in meter. One board in the Spliced shell holds 1,800 MHz at a 35 degree inlet on its own two fans: one shell, one afternoon, a thermocouple and the power log. Six in a tray hold it with the tray's fans running. Eighty-four stay within a 20 degree rise at the hot-aisle face. A full pod's joules per correct answer at the wall. Nothing is measured yet, and every capped run so far held its clock without a temperature on record.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="etched">
    <div class="etched-grid solo">
      <figure class="wide"><img src="../assets/img/node-row.webp" alt="Six pods of nodes side by side in a hot-aisle row" loading="lazy"></figure>
    </div>
  </section>
